 signals. An opaque scalar without its required computable payload is rejected
 by Serde and returns `Problem::ParseError` through the convenience readers.
+Valid named symbolic certificates may omit their lazy payload; fact queries,
+normal forms, and subsequent arithmetic reconstruct it from the certificate
+without changing the serialized expression. Existing payloads retain their
+proofs and caches. Each value retains at most one reconstructed graph until
+the value is dropped; its size follows the certificate and is not bounded by
+a byte limit. Primitive caching or reconstruction allocates one private cache
+record on first use; an unused cache occupies one inline atomic pointer.
 Validation of mathematical domains inside present expression graphs remains
